## Solution

**Rewrite the general term.** For $x > 1$ (so that $\ln x > 0$), use $a^b = e^{b\ln a}$:

$$
(\ln x)^{\ln n} = e^{\ln n \cdot \ln(\ln x)} = n^{\ln(\ln x)}.
$$

Hence

$$
\frac{1}{(\ln x)^{\ln n}} = \frac{1}{n^{\ln(\ln x)}}.
$$

**Reduce to a $p$-series.** The series becomes

$$
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{(\ln x)^{\ln n}} = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^{\,\ln(\ln x)}}.
$$

This is a $p$-series with $p = \ln(\ln x)$, which converges if and only if $p > 1$.

**Solve the inequality.** We need

$$
\ln(\ln x) > 1 \iff \ln x > e \iff x > e^{e}.
$$

**Check the domain/excluded cases.**
- For $0 < x \le 1$: $\ln x \le 0$, and the expression $(\ln x)^{\ln n}$ is not a well-defined positive real base raised to a real exponent, so the series is not real-valued.
- For $x = e$: $\ln(\ln x) = \ln 1 = 0$, giving the harmonic series $\sum 1/n$, which diverges — already excluded by $x > e^e$.
- For $1 < x < e^e$ (with $x \ne e$): $\ln(\ln x) \le 1$ (in fact $\ln(\ln x) < 1$ when $x < e^e$, and $\ln(\ln x) < 0$ when $x < e$), so the $p$-series diverges.

**Conclusion.** The series converges precisely when

$$
\boxed{x > e^{e}}.
$$

